 ],
 "title": "Mathematical Theory of the Transformer, Volume I: Foundations, Rationality, and the Incremental Transformer",
 "byline": "Giansalvo Cirrincione · manuscript prepared for MIT Press, 15 May 2026 (Zenodo DOI 10.5281/zenodo.20199413)",
 "note": "A research monograph with very fine sectioning, so summaries and terms are given per chapter; each section is listed with its PDF page range. Concluding-remarks sections and the four appendices (mathematical background, notation, experimental pipeline, Schatten deflation) are not broken out. Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "A mathematical theory of the Transformer’s attention mechanism, built around the bilinear score matrix M = W_Q W_Kᵀ and its split into symmetric and antisymmetric parts. After setting up embeddings, positional encoding, layer norm, multi-head attention and BERT (with a SARS-CoV-2 classification case study), it proves results about reciprocity, directionality and post-softmax persistence, classifies attention heads into eight canonical local regimes and global “signature geographies”, and models information flow with a motor formalism of forward and backward dynamics. It studies how signatures emerge during training (orbits, sharpness, activation, silence), defines notions of head redundancy and design principles, and proposes a “Rationalised Transformer” and an incremental growth controller (INCRT) with guarantees, spectral gate analysis, experiments on proteomes, control and materials design, and a recommended anchored multi-head bilinear gated (A-MHB-G) architecture."
}